# Build context for packages/fleet/tracy-fleet-operation/dsh/Dockerfile.
# The image needs vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness (source, no node_modules) and packages/* (committed lib).
**/node_modules
.git
# Secrets never belong in a build context: every file here is readable by every build step and
# lands in the daemon's cache even when no COPY names it.
.env*
!.env*.example
.npmrc
*.pem
*.key
.dev-logs
.superpowers
tch
infra
docs
tasks
packages/dev
packages/web
packages/desktop
# THIS FILE NOW SERVES MORE THAN ONE DOCKERFILE. It was written for the fleet dsh image above, but
# `deploy/compose.yml` also builds `apps/tracy-web/Dockerfile` with `context: ..` (the repo root),
# and Docker has exactly one .dockerignore per context — there is no per-Dockerfile override here.
# So the blanket `packages/dev`/`packages/web`/`packages/desktop` exclusions above, meant only for
# the fleet image, silently ate two `dist/` directories `apps/tracy-web/Dockerfile` COPYs in (Plan
# 4 Tasks 2 and 3) — a `COPY` that names a real, existing path still lands nothing, with no error
# at build time (`CopyIgnoredFile`), because the file never left the host in the first place.
# Widening `packages/web`'s exclusion above without reading this comment breaks one of these again.
!packages/web/tracy-harness/dist
!packages/web/tracy-harness/dist/**
!packages/web/tracy-design-page/dist
!packages/web/tracy-design-page/dist/**
!packages/web/tracy-wo-find-page/dist
!packages/web/tracy-wo-find-page/dist/**
# ── vendor: IN the context since the fleet image, and each one only in part ───────────────────
#
# Until 06/09 every line below was an exclusion: the dev image carried five Tracy plugins and got
# dsh's own bundles from the submodule. The fleet image carries what a lane carries, and most of
# those packages are somebody else's code that this repo builds from source (LAW #5) instead of
# pulling from npm.tracy.ai. So the sources come in — and nothing else does.
#
# `**/node_modules` above already strips the heavy half (`dsh-better-sidebar` 572 MB,
# `dsh-passwords` 330 MB on disk here, almost all of it installed dependencies). What is left is
# per-package: documentation, test corpora and demo assets that no build step reads.
vendor/*/*/docs
vendor/*/*/.git
vendor/tracy/dsh-better-sidebar/tests
vendor/tracy/dsh-passwords/test
# Their BUILD OUTPUT, which the image builds itself (`pnpm build` / `npm run build` in the Dockerfile).
# Both are gitignored, and both exist on any CI runner because the root `pnpm install` runs each
# fork's `prepare`. Sent into the context they carried each runner's own copy — and broke the layer
# cache on every build that landed on a different runner than the last one: measured 15/09, a
# same-commit rebuild on the CI box reinstalled and rebuilt dsh-better-sidebar (47 s) because its
# `COPY` layer hashed a `lib/` another runner had built.
vendor/tracy/dsh-better-sidebar/lib
vendor/tracy/dsh-passwords/dist
# `skills/` is the whole reason mattpocock-skills is in the context (the deck that used to carry
# them was dropped 06/09); its own repository furniture is not read by any build step.
vendor/tracy/mattpocock-skills/.agents
vendor/tracy/mattpocock-skills/scripts
vendor/tracy/mattpocock-skills/package-lock.json
# `systems/` and `catalog.json` are what /opt/tracy serves; `packages/` is the upstream monorepo
# that BUILDS them (38 MB), and nothing in the image reads it.
vendor/tracy/od-design-system/packages
# Refero (447 MB: source pages, exports, translated DESIGN.md) stays out...
vendor/tracy/refero
# ...except what the tracy-web image's design system doors read for EVERY system they offer
# (`apps/tracy-web/src/lib/ds-slot.js` referoLibrary, Refero only since 12/10/2026): the catalogue, and
# of each system the two files `loadProfile` reads (~30 MB in the context; the Dockerfile's `dslib` stage
# keeps only the offered systems', ~24 MB). The card pictures are Tracy's own thumbnails, one per offered
# system (`packages/design/tracy-ds-slot/refero-thumbs/`, 10 MB), not the snapshot's screenshots (151 MB).
!vendor/tracy/refero/catalog.json
!vendor/tracy/refero/systems/*/design-system.json
!vendor/tracy/refero/systems/*/manifest.json
# Bravada (545 files, 33 MB — 429 of them demo photography), vendored 10/09 as the tree Tracy forks
# its EmDash template out of. Same reason as the line below: the dsh image runs none of it.
vendor/emdash/emdash-theme-bravada
# The two Tracy templates derived from it — one per design system, 548 files each, and the
# same reason again: the dsh image runs neither. They reach a customer through the EmDash
# site image, never through this build context.
vendor/emdash/emdash-theme-tracy-editorial
vendor/emdash/emdash-theme-tracy-coral
# EmDash's own monorepo (4152 files, 67 MB), vendored 10/09 so its Dockerfile and templates can be
# read here instead of on a droplet. It is the CMS a CUSTOMER's site runs, never anything this
# image runs: no COPY in dsh/Dockerfile names it, so every byte it adds to the context is a slower
# build on the host for no change in the image. Whole directory, not a part — unlike the vendors
# above, none of it is read by any build step.
vendor/emdash/emdash
# vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/website stays in the context: tsconfig.host.json includes
# website/**/*.ts, so `pnpm build:official` cannot type-check without it (80 KB).
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/docs
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/.git
# dsh's build output from the host tree. None of it is tracked (`git ls-files` finds no lib/,
# dist/ or .tsbuildinfo under the submodule), and a stale copy is worse than none: `tsc -b`
# trusts the .tsbuildinfo, skips the work, and tsdown then bundles the previous commit's types.
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/**/lib
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/**/dist
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/**/*.tsbuildinfo
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/.dsh-build
vendor/tracy/deepseek-harness/.typecheck
# The open Website Opportunities package goes into the tracy-web image as `node_modules/website-opportunities`;
# its tests, fixtures and public-repository furniture are read by no build step.
packages/wo/website-opportunities/test
packages/wo/website-opportunities/docs
packages/wo/website-opportunities/.github
